alwaysApply: true
description: Avoid superficial fallbacks and hardcoding when changing functionality
---
### Functional changes: no superficial fallbacks or hardcoding

- Avoid superficial fallbacks
  - Do not silently bypass failures with implicit defaults or broad catch-all handlers.
  - Prefer explicit validation and raise actionable errors. See `fail-fast-coding.mdc`.
  - Do not add hidden auto-retry loops or quiet degradations unless justified, documented, and test-covered.

- Avoid hardcoding
  - Avoid magic numbers, inline constants, and environment-dependent paths.
  - Prefer configuration, typed parameters, or centralized constants with a single source of truth.
  - If a default is necessary, centralize it and document rationale, scope, and removal plan.

- Design for clarity and extensibility
  - Prefer dependency injection and clear interfaces over scattered conditionals or flag-based branching.
  - Make behavior discoverable via configuration/specs instead of ad-hoc code paths.

- Validation and rollout
  - Add unit/integration tests for new/changed behavior, including failure paths and edge cases.
  - If a temporary guard is required, use a feature flag with explicit owner and removal timeline.

- When in doubt
  - If an instruction requires a fallback or hardcode, pause and request confirmation per `confirm-on-concerns.mdc`.
  - Accompany every edit with a concise explanation per `edit-explanation.mdc`.
